{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Problem\n\n`_PROVIDER_PREFIXES` in `src/rebar/llm/config.py` maps the `gemini` prefix to the provider name\n`google_genai`, but no runtime registry knows that name, so any bare `gemini-*` model id resolves to\na provider that cannot be constructed and dies at call time.\n\nReproduced on this base (worktree at `origin/main`, `d72c477`):\n\n    infer_provider('gemini-2.5-pro')                 -> 'google_genai'\n    'google_genai' in _pydantic_ai_known_providers() -> False\n    'google_genai' in KNOWN_PROVIDER_NAMES           -> False\n    ProviderSession(LLMConfig())._builders           -> ['anthropic', 'bedrock']\n    ProviderSession.is_resolvable('google_genai')    -> False\n    ProviderSession.provider_factory('google_genai') -> LLMConfigError:\n        unknown provider 'google_genai'; registered providers: ['anthropic', 'bedrock']\n\npydantic-ai 1.107.1 recognises `google`, `google-cloud`, `google-gla`, `google-vertex`, `vertexai`\nand `gateway/google-cloud`. It does NOT recognise `google_genai`.\n\n### Which path actually breaks\n\nThere are two composition sites, and only one of them is broken — this matters for where the test\ngoes:\n\n- `anthropic_model._pai_model()` (used by `runner.run()` for a BARE `cfg.model`) passes the inferred\n  name through a second, private table, `_PAI_PROVIDER_PREFIX`, which happens to translate\n  `google_genai -> google-gla`. That path accidentally survives.\n- `model_classes._resolve_target()` — the model-class / ladder path (`trivial = { model =\n  \"gemini-2.5-pro\" }`, fallback chains, `resolve_model_string`) — uses `infer_provider`'s result\n  VERBATIM and composes `google_genai:gemini-2.5-pro`. `_pai_model` then returns that string\n  unchanged because it is already qualified, and provider construction raises. This is the\n  end-to-end failure and is what the RED test must exercise.\n\nThe failure is at CALL time, not config time: an operator who configures a `gemini-*` model gets a\nplausible-looking config that fails only when a gate actually runs.\n\n## Design decision\n\n**Map `gemini` to `google`.** `google` is pydantic-ai's current canonical provider name for the\nGemini Developer API; `google:gemini-*` ids appear directly in `known_model_names()`, and `google`\nis already a member of both `KNOWN_PROVIDER_NAMES` and `_pydantic_ai_known_providers()`, so\n`ProviderSession.is_resolvable('google')` is True and the model is left on the existing deferred\nlazy-string path (rebar registers no builder for it, exactly as for `openai` without a `base_url`).\nBuilding it still requires the optional `google-genai` package, and pydantic-ai's own ImportError\nalready names the extra to install — that is the same contract every other delegate provider has,\nnot a new failure mode.\n\nRejected alternatives:\n\n- `google-gla` / `google-vertex` / `vertexai` — resolvable, but pydantic-ai 1.107.1 emits a\n  deprecation warning for these prefixes and removes them in v2.0, so choosing one would\n  re-introduce a scheduled break.\n- Dropping the `gemini` prefix entirely so a bare `gemini-*` id fails at config time — this yields a\n  WORSE outcome for an operator who has the Google extra installed: a model that pydantic-ai can\n  serve today would stop being usable via a bare id.\n- Adding `google_genai` to `KNOWN_PROVIDER_NAMES` (what 7b22's plan proposes for a back-compat test)\n  — rejected: it makes a name that neither pydantic-ai nor rebar can build a first-class member, and\n  `ProviderSession` would still refuse it. 7b22 must not lock that choice in.\n\n`anthropic_model._PAI_PROVIDER_PREFIX` is deliberately NOT touched. Its `google_genai` key becomes\nunreachable from inference but is still a valid explicit-`model_provider` back-compat shim, and\n`capabilities.py` documents its `google-gla` output as load-bearing. Changing it is a separate\nconcern from making the inference table agree with the registries.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/config.py` — `_PROVIDER_PREFIXES`: `(\"gemini\", \"google_genai\")` becomes\n  `(\"gemini\", \"google\")`; update the two comments that restate the old mapping (the\n  `KNOWN_PROVIDER_NAMES` preamble and the `LLMConfig.model_provider` comment).\n- `tests/unit/test_provider_qualifier.py` — replace `test_the_inference_path_is_not_validated`\n  (which pins the defect as deliberately out of scope) with the fixed expectation; add the\n  whole-table invariant and the regression pins.\n- `tests/interfaces/store/test_llm_framework.py` — `test_infer_provider` pins\n  `infer_provider(\"gemini-2.5-pro\") == \"google_genai\"`; update to the resolvable name.\n\n## Test plan\n\n1. RED first, on the end-to-end mechanism: assert\n   `model_classes._resolve_target(\"gemini-2.5-pro\", None)` composes a provider string that\n   `ProviderSession(LLMConfig()).is_resolvable(...)` accepts. On unfixed code this fails with\n   `google_genai`.\n2. AC2 invariant, parameterized over the WHOLE `_PROVIDER_PREFIXES` table (not just the gemini\n   entry): every mapped provider name is in `_pydantic_ai_known_providers()` or in\n   `ProviderSession._builders`, so a future added prefix cannot reintroduce the class of defect.\n3. AC3 regression pins: `claude-* -> anthropic`, `gpt-*/chatgpt-* -> openai`, an already-qualified\n   `google-gla:gemini-2.5-flash` still passes through unchanged, an unknown id still infers `None`.\n4. Mutation-check in both directions: revert the one-line production change and confirm the new\n   tests go RED; restore and confirm GREEN.\n5. Gates by exit code: `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and the default `pytest` selection.\n\n## Risk and rollback\n\nOne-line data change in a lookup table plus test updates; no control flow changes. Blast radius is\nlimited to model ids that begin with `gemini`, which today cannot work at all. Rollback is reverting\nthe single commit.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- AC1: A bare `gemini-*` model id resolves, through both `infer_provider` and\n  `model_classes._resolve_target`, to a provider name that `ProviderSession.is_resolvable` accepts.\n- AC2: No value in `_PROVIDER_PREFIXES` maps to a provider name absent from both\n  `_pydantic_ai_known_providers()` and `ProviderSession._builders`. A test pins this for the WHOLE\n  table, not just the gemini entry, so the next added prefix cannot reintroduce the class of defect.\n- AC3: Inference for `claude-*`, `gpt-*`/`chatgpt-*`, already-qualified ids, and unknown ids is\n  unchanged, pinned by test.\n- AC4: `make lint` and `make typecheck` pass by exit code; the default pytest selection is green."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785705574252668001,"uuid":"3ef663dc-24ba-40b1-a251-52eb40b4d72c"}